    "result_text": "The build is green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required validating the E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting with 400 otherwise.\n\nI implemented this in `TelephoneE164.toE164(...)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java`):\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTH` map of country code \u2192 required national-digit count (`\"1\"` \u2192 10, `\"61\"` \u2192 9).\n- After the existing digit/length sanitisation, the code now matches the leading country code and returns `null` (which `ValidateOwnerFields` turns into a `\"telephone\"` 400 error) when the national part is the wrong length for that country.\n- Unknown country codes fall back to the general 8\u201315 total-digit rule, preserving prior behaviour (e.g. the `+64` example).\n\nThe prefixes are mutually exclusive, so match order is irrelevant. The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the full test suite builds green.",
